    '**⚠️ SITE SEPARATES STRIDOR FROM STERTOR, NOT PHASE — both can be purely inspiratory.** Pitch is the clue: stertor is **large, floppy, low-tension soft tissue** flapping in a wide pharynx; stridor is **taut mucosa over cartilage in a narrow rigid segment** *(acoustic reasoning not taken from the course material)*. Stertor is always inspiratory because the pharynx is a **collapsible tube with no cartilage** — inspiration drops the pressure inside and partly collapses it, expiration blows it open. That is also the mechanism of snoring and OSA.',
