    '',
    '> **⚠️⚠️ THE SINGLE MOST DANGEROUS CLINICAL INVERSION IN THIS CHAPTER: A PATIENT WHO WAS NOISY AND HAS GONE QUIET HAS EITHER CLEARED OR STOPPED VENTILATING.** Look at the patient, not the monitor. Pink, calm, easy breathing = improved. Pale, exhausted, cyanosed, **bradycardic** = peri-arrest — and `L6` files cyanosis and bradycardia as its two **late signs**. **A fading stridor in a tiring patient is worsening obstruction.** *(Interpretation not taken from the course material; the late signs are `L6`’s.)*',
