    '**⚠️ AND A FIXED, RIGID NARROWING OBSTRUCTS IN BOTH PHASES.** The subglottis is enclosed by the **cricoid — the only complete cartilaginous ring in the airway** — so a scar or tumour there can be neither pulled open on inspiration nor splinted open on expiration. **That gives an inference worth more than the list itself: BIPHASIC STRIDOR MEANS A FIXED LESION, NOT A FLOPPY ONE — so it means imaging and endoscopy, not observation.** A dynamic lesion varies with phase: laryngomalacia is inspiratory and worse supine, tracheomalacia expiratory.',
